{=============================================================================
  Rigid tank with a liquid-vapour water mixture
  CoolSolve Library model CSL-0044
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  A 1 m^3 rigid vessel contains 4 kg of water as a liquid-vapour equilibrium
  mixture at a given pressure (6 bar). The model computes:
    a. the masses of liquid and vapour (through the quality of the mixture);
    b. the volumes occupied by the liquid and by the vapour.
  Classic introductory exercise on saturation properties of Water.

  Inputs : Vol (tank volume), m (mass of water), p (pressure)
  Outputs: x (quality), m_l, m_v, Vol_l, Vol_v, v_l, v_v, T
  Units  : SI - temperatures in °C, pressures in Pa, energies in J
  Source : ULiège, course "Thermodynamique appliquée" (MECA0002), repetition 1,
           exercise 1 (EES file R1_E1_2022.EES) - see README.md
=============================================================================}

"!Data"
Vol = 1.0 [m^3]                  "tank volume [m^3]"
m = 4.0 [kg]                     "total mass of water [kg]"
p = 600E3 [Pa]                   "600 kPa in the original [Pa]"

"!a. Masses of liquid and vapour"

Vol = Vol_v + Vol_l              "the volume is shared between liquid and vapour"
m = m_v + m_l                    "likewise the mass splits into a liquid part and a vapour part"

{The link between mass and volume is the specific volume, read from the
saturation tables of Water.}

v_avg = Vol/m                    "average specific volume of the mixture [m^3/kg]"

x = QUALITY(Water, v=v_avg, P=p) "quality of the mixture [-]"

m_v = x*m                        "vapour mass; m_l follows from m = m_v + m_l (no separate
                                  equation needed, as in the original) [kg]"

"!b. Volumes of liquid and vapour"

Vol_l = v_l*m_l                  "liquid volume [m^3]"

{The equation Vol_v = v_v*m_v is not needed, as in the original: Vol_v already
 follows from Vol = Vol_v + Vol_l.}

v_l = VOLUME(Water, x=0, P=p)    "specific volume of saturated liquid [m^3/kg]"
v_v = VOLUME(Water, x=1, P=p)    "specific volume of saturated vapour [m^3/kg]"

"!For information"
T = T_SAT(Water, P=p)            "saturation temperature of water at p [°C]"
